course: dictionary
lesson: 7116
title: Peek and the clues around it
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can be a naming word (a noun) or an action word (a verb). Peek can be both. «Take a peek» uses peek as a noun, a quick look. «No peeking» is about an action you must not do.
explain: To peek means to look at something quickly or secretly, often when you should not. «Peeking under the blindfold in blind man's buff is soon seen, and then the others won't let you be "it" any more.» The clue is the blindfold: the player is looking when they should not be.
explain: Peek can also mean to just show a little. «The sun was peeking out from behind the clouds» does not mean anyone is cheating. It means only a little of the sun can be seen.
explain: Swap in your meaning and check it. Try "looking quickly or secretly" in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Peek is a verb (peek, peeks, peeked, peeking) and a noun (a peek).
fact: «Peek» means to look at something quickly or secretly, often when you should not.
fact: «A peek» means a quick, short look.
fact: «Peek out» means to just show a little, like the sun peeking out from behind clouds.
fact: «Sneak peek» means a first look at something new, and «peek-a-boo» is a game for babies.
exercise: «Peeking at presents usually spoils the surprise for you, not only for the giver.» What does peeking mean here? | *Looking quickly or secretly at something you should not | Wrapping a present carefully | Shaking a box to guess what is inside | Giving someone a gift
exercise: «The sun was peeking out from behind the clouds.» What does peeking out mean? | Shining very brightly | *Just showing a little | Falling down fast | Hiding completely
exercise: In the phrase «take a peek», what kind of word is peek? | A verb, an action word like run | *A noun, a quick look | A full stop | An adjective, describing a colour
test: «A peek is cheating even if you'd have got most of it right.» What is the main reason peeking in a test is cheating? | Peeking is only cheating if you get every answer wrong | *Peeking breaks the rules, even if you would have got most of it right | Peeking is fine if you are good at the test | Peeking only matters to the teacher
test: «Rules agreed before a game are a kind of promise between players, so changing them... breaks that promise.» What is a rule agreed before a game like? | *A kind of promise between players | Something you can change when you are losing | A prize for the winner | A list of the squares on the board
exam: According to «Cheating, like peeking in hide-and-seek, moving extra squares or changing the rules halfway because you are losing, spoils the game for everyone.», which of these is cheating? | *Peeking in hide-and-seek | Hiding behind a tree | Calling "ready or not" loudly | Counting to ten slowly
